The GRAVITY acquisition camera utilizes a single $4096 \times 4096$ $~1$ $\mu m$ HgCdTe sensor to accomplish multiple functions on the Very Large Telescope Interferometer (VLTI). These functions are fulfilled via distinct data streams: one for overall telescope image and others for specific subarrays. These data streams use independent readout modes to optimize various characteristics in line with the algorithm's requirements. A customized real-time software framework facilitates the end-to-end data flow and manages the diverse data streams. It leverages the Data Access Layer to provide a unified interface for the camera and other VLTI components. We describe the specific technology used in the GRAVITY acquisition camera, the distinct data streams created during the on-sky operation, and the particular approaches applied to data processing and demosaicing. We also discuss the various consequences of choosing different approaches to data processing. A particular focus is given to the techniques used to optimize the various parameters of the alternative readout modes for real-time data access during the on-sky operation. We briefly outline the fundamental principles of interferometry and the primary objectives of the GRAVITY project. We then delve into the major advantages of using a high-speed camera for the testbed of various optical technologies and the contributions of the camera to advancing the field of VLTI. We conclude with the future plans for the camera, specifically the proposed enhancements that will further increase its value to the scientific objectives of the GRAVITY project and the potential applications to the Atacama Large Millimeter Array (ALMA). \\

--- \section{Introduction} 

The GRAVITY acquisition camera is a cutting-edge $~1$ $\mu m$ HgCdTe sensor-based instrument designed to facilitate various functions on the Very Large Telescope Interferometer (VLTI). These functions are fulfilled via distinct data streams: one for overall telescope image and others for specific subarrays. A customized real-time software framework, described later, manages the diverse data streams. We will describe the specific technology used in the camera, the particular approaches applied to data processing, and the various consequences of choosing different options. The testbed of various optical technologies is a major advantage of using a high-speed camera which will further increase its value to the scientific objectives of the GRAVITY project and the potential applications to the Atacama Large Millimeter Array (ALMA). \\

